The three attachments for your next tenants' meeting are named
\texttt{Hall plan.txt}, \texttt{Hall plan (revised).txt}, and
\texttt{Hall plan [draft].txt}. In a file manager you can click on a
whole name. At the terminal you have to tell the shell where the name
ends. And when a name contains brackets, you have to tell it whether
those brackets describe a pattern or belong to the name. Both jobs are
done by quoting. Neither job requires renaming the files.

\begin{objectives}
\item Pass a literal name as one argument, even if the name contains spaces.
\item Choose between single quotes, double quotes and a backslash.
\item Distinguish shell quoting from the rules of the command that receives the argument.
\end{objectives}

\section{See where the shell cuts}
\label{sec:25-boundaries}
\index{quoting}
\index{arguments!boundaries}
\index{printf@\texttt{printf}}

In Chapter~\ref{ch:13} you met arguments, the separate pieces of
information you hand to a command. A blank between unquoted words
normally tells the shell to begin another word; a blank inside quotes
can be part of a word. Try the following inside a fresh \texttt{sh}
session. Type \texttt{sh} and press Enter to start one; type
\texttt{exit} at the end to return to your usual shell. These first
commands neither read nor write any files.

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' Hall plan.txt
printf '<%s>\n' 'Hall plan.txt'
\end{terminal}

The first argument to \texttt{printf} is its format: print a literal
opening angle bracket, replace \texttt{\%s} with one supplied string,
print a closing angle bracket, and end the line at \texttt{\textbackslash n}.
For this format, \texttt{printf} repeats the operation for each
remaining argument. The two commands above therefore print:

\begin{terminaloutput}
<Hall>
<plan.txt>
<Hall plan.txt>
\end{terminaloutput}

The brackets here are display marks, not part of the name. They even
make a blank at the beginning or end of a value visible. This trick is
more useful than \texttt{echo}, whose usual space-separated output can
make two arguments look like one. One limitation: a name that contains
a newline can produce more than one line of output inside its brackets.
The display tells you about the boundaries, not everything about the
bytes. For today's one-line names it is enough.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-25/boundaries.tikz}
\caption{A blank can separate arguments or belong to an argument.}
\label{fig:25-boundaries}
\VisualDescription{The unquoted form feeds Hall and plan.txt into separate slots. The quoted form feeds the two words and their intervening space into a single slot.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:25-boundaries} shows what the receiving command sees.
It does not have to guess where the name ends or strip off quote marks:
the shell has already determined the boundaries and removed the marks
used for quoting. This also explains a common surprise. If you get a
message about a file named \texttt{Hall} rather than \texttt{Hall
plan.txt}, the file may not be missing at all. The command was asked
to open a different name. Correct the boundary before checking
permissions or rebuilding the folder.

\section{Three tools for the same blank}
\label{sec:25-forms}

All three lines below send the same argument. In the third line the
backslash protects only the immediately following space; it does not
make the rest of the word literal.

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' 'Hall plan.txt'
printf '<%s>\n' "Hall plan.txt"
printf '<%s>\n' Hall\ plan.txt
\end{terminal}

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.1in}YY}
\toprule
Tool & What it protects & What still needs attention \\
\midrule
Single quotes & Everything between the two marks is literal to the shell. & A single quote cannot appear inside that pair, even after a backslash. \\
Double quotes & Spaces and glob characters inside are protected from splitting and matching. & Dollar signs and backticks can still introduce substitutions; some backslashes still have a special role. \\
Backslash outside quotes & Protects the next character; useful for one blank or one literal mark. & A backslash directly before a newline joins the lines rather than inserting a newline. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Prefer single quotes when you know the exact text you want to pass.
Double quotes are useful where you intend a substitution, such as
looking up your home directory with \cmd|"$HOME"|. In that spelling the
shell looks up the value and keeps the result together. In
\cmd|'$HOME'| you pass five literal characters instead. Use an
invented value for this demonstration so you do not have to show a
real account path. The line beginning \texttt{meeting=} saves a value
for the current session. The next chapter shows how substitution fits
into the larger sequence; Chapter~\ref{ch:27} develops variables.

\begin{terminal}
meeting='Tuesday setup'
printf '<%s>\n' '$meeting' "$meeting"
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{<\$meeting>} followed by
\texttt{<Tuesday setup>}. The question to ask before each quote pair
is not ``Which kind do I normally use?'' but ``Which characters do I
want the shell to act on?'' The distinction matters for a title like
\texttt{\$5 deposit}; the dollar sign belongs to the title, and single
quotes say so. If you combine a variable with a literal price, leave
the variable in double quotes and use single quotes around the
price. The following example puts the two pieces side by side with
no gap outside the quotes, which yields one argument:

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' "$meeting"': $5 deposit'
\end{terminal}

The space after the colon is inside the second pair. Contrast
\cmd|"$meeting" ': $5 deposit'|, where the unquoted blank between the
pairs creates two arguments. The resulting lines from the two versions
look similar if passed to a command that joins its arguments for
display. The angle-bracket probe tells them apart.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-25/segments.tikz}
\caption{Two adjacent quoted pieces form one word.}
\label{fig:25-segments}
\VisualDescription{The first piece supplies Tuesday setup from a variable. The second supplies a literal colon, space and price. With no unquoted space between the pieces, the shell joins them into one argument.}
\end{figure}

An empty quoted piece can be a whole argument in its own right. Use
the following experiment to compare a missing argument with an
empty one. This time never remove the final \texttt{end} marker:
without any value arguments at all, \texttt{printf} would still
apply the format with an empty string, hiding the difference.

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' '' end
printf '<%s>\n' end
\end{terminal}

The first run shows \texttt{<>} and \texttt{<end>}; the second
shows only \texttt{<end>}. This distinction comes up with optional
fields. Leaving out a second argument may tell a command to use its
usual value, while supplying an empty one may explicitly request a
blank. The exact decision belongs to the receiving command.

\section{When the name includes a quote}

If you want to print \texttt{Marta's notes.txt}, you can wrap
it in double quotes, because no dollars or substitutions are needed
in this example and the apostrophe has no special role inside double
quotes. A second solution is to put both halves in single quotes and
insert the apostrophe with a backslash \emph{outside} those quotes.
The latter looks busier but is useful when the rest must stay
literal, such as a name with both a dollar and an apostrophe:

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' "Marta's notes.txt"
printf '<%s>\n' 'Marta'\''s $5 notes.txt'
\end{terminal}

Read the second spelling as three pieces: \cmd|'Marta'|,
\cmd|\'|, and \cmd|'s $5 notes.txt'|. There is no separating
unquoted space. Just as with Figure~\ref{fig:25-segments}, the result
is one argument. A plain apostrophe and a typographic right quote
may appear interchangeable in a printed announcement, but the shell
recognizes only the straight ASCII punctuation as quoting syntax.
If pasting from a word processor gives a puzzling error, retype the
quote marks directly at the prompt.

Missing the closing quote has a different symptom: the shell often
shows a continuation prompt, such as \texttt{>}, instead of running
the command. The open quote could hold further lines, so Enter does
not finish the command. If you did not intend a newline in the
argument, use \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C} to abandon the input and start over.
A continuation prompt is not an instruction to type another
\texttt{>} on the next line.

\begin{dialoguebox}{The file has no quotes}
\Brian{The flyer doesn't have quote marks in its name. Won't adding them make the shell look for a different file?}
\Stephen{Not if you use them as quoting syntax. They tell the shell which characters belong in one argument.}
\Brian{But I just printed a name with an apostrophe in it. The apostrophe survived.}
\Stephen{Because another layer protected it. A single quote inside double quotes is data, not the start of another quoted section.}
\Brian{So if I want a literal double quote, I can protect it with single quotes?}
\Stephen{Yes. Or escape that one character outside quotes. The point is to decide which marks the shell reads as instructions and which it passes on.}
\DialogueConclusion{Quoting marks and quoted data are different.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Read a fixture by its exact name}
\label{sec:25-fixture}

Start with a new practice directory. The \texttt{-d} option of
\texttt{mktemp} asks for a directory rather than a file; this
convenient form is available on common GNU and BSD implementations.
The first line stores the pathname printed by the utility. Use the
lines individually: proceed only if the first command succeeds and
the second prints the new practice directory. If \texttt{mktemp}
is absent or reports an error, stop and make a fresh practice folder
with the method in Chapter~\ref{ch:12} instead. Never practice the
file-writing lines in a folder with real documents.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d)
cd "$practice" && pwd
\end{terminal}

Here \cmd|$(...)| captures the command's standard output, and
\cmd|&&| runs the second command only if the first succeeds.
Create two files with different contents, then try both reads.
The \texttt{>} writes the left command's output to the named file;
an existing file would be truncated. That is why the empty
practice directory matters. Stop if any creation command fails.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Send for comments' > 'Hall [draft].txt'
printf '%s\n' 'Approved' > Hall\ d.txt
cat 'Hall [draft].txt'
cat Hall\ [draft].txt
\end{terminal}

The quoted read prints \texttt{Send for comments}. The second
read prints \texttt{Approved}. It protects the space but leaves the
brackets active, so the shell matches one character from the
bracket expression (in this directory, \texttt{d}). The output
would change if you added \texttt{Hall a.txt}. If there were no
matches, a POSIX shell normally leaves the original pattern
unchanged, which would make this example appear to work by accident.
Other interactive shells may stop on a nonmatching glob. Build
tests with both the name you want and plausible alternatives;
success in an almost-empty folder can be misleading.

\begin{terminal}
grep -F '[draft]' <<'SIGNS'
Hall [draft]
Hall d
SIGNS
\end{terminal}

In that optional read-only example, \cmd|<<'SIGNS'| supplies the
lines beneath it as standard input up to, but not including, the
line containing just \texttt{SIGNS}. This is a \emph{here document}.
Quoting the delimiter also prevents substitutions within this
input. The quotes around \cmd|'[draft]'| protect the brackets from
the shell; \texttt{-F} separately tells \texttt{grep} to interpret
its operand as fixed text rather than as a regular expression.
Expected output: \texttt{Hall [draft]}. Those protections are
independent. If you remove \texttt{-F}, the quoted operand remains
one argument but \texttt{grep} matches one letter from the
bracket expression. Chapter~\ref{ch:19} covers that second language.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-25/layers.tikz}
\caption{The shell and the utility interpret different things.}
\label{fig:25-layers}
\VisualDescription{First the shell turns a quoted token into one bracketed argument. Next grep interprets that argument: with the fixed-string option, square brackets are plain text.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:25-layers} is a general debugging tool. If the
wrong files are found, ask who selected them. If the right files
are read but the wrong lines match, ask what the search utility
does with its pattern. If a name begins with a hyphen, quoting
it does not prevent the utility from mistaking it for an option.
A relative file path beginning with \texttt{./} makes the first
character a dot instead. Try the following in your fixture only:

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'This is a filename' > './-n'
cat './-n'
\end{terminal}

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.3in}YY}
\toprule
Symptom & Layer to check & Useful first change \\
\midrule
Two unexpected operands & Shell word boundaries & Quote the whole pathname or inspect it with the argument probe. \\
A different matching file & Shell globbing & Quote the bracket or wildcard characters you intend literally. \\
Correct pattern text, wrong matches & Search utility & Request fixed strings when no regular expression is intended. \\
A file looks like an option & Receiving utility & Try the pathname with a leading dot-slash. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

One more layer is sometimes present: a command can pass text to
\emph{another} shell. Quoting the first shell sees cannot settle
what the second shell will do if that shell receives a string to
interpret anew. This is one reason to avoid constructing command
strings from filenames. When you just want to pass a filename to
a utility you already know, supply it as its own argument instead.
Chapter~\ref{ch:30} puts that design to work.

\section{Use a format for display and quotes for data}
\label{sec:25-format}

The first operand to \texttt{printf} is a tiny language all its
own. A title containing a percent sign is ordinary text to the
shell, but has a special meaning if put where \texttt{printf}
expects its format. Here are two safe commands to illustrate the
distinction. Neither touches the fixture files.

\begin{terminal}
title='100% ready'
printf '%s\n' "$title"
printf 'Flyer status (%s)\n' "$title"
\end{terminal}

The first prints \texttt{100\% ready}, and the second wraps
that text in a longer sentence. The title is data both times.
If you instead choose \cmd|printf "$title"|, the shell still
preserves the title as one argument, but the receiving utility
may see a malformed format or print something different.
The correct remedy is not another pair of quotes. It is to put
the user's text after a format you chose. This is the same
separation you saw with \texttt{grep}, where requesting fixed
strings was a decision at the utility layer. A good read-only
probe makes both decisions explicit: pass one string, display
it as one string.

Now suppose you have a file name from a message and want to
use it as a redirection destination. In the following read-only
example the destination goes in the opposite direction:
\texttt{<} opens the selected file as standard input for
\texttt{cat}. This lets you identify the argument to
\texttt{cat} separately from the name opened by the shell.

\begin{terminal}
draft='Hall [draft].txt'
cat < "$draft"
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Send for comments}, as earlier. This time
\texttt{cat} receives no filename argument; the shell opens
the selected file and connects it to \texttt{cat}'s input.
The command reads the same bytes as before. If the name is
wrong, the shell can report a redirection error before
\texttt{cat} even starts. For an output redirection with
\texttt{>}, the corresponding implication is more important:
the shell may already have emptied the selected existing file
before you know whether the command can do its job. Test your
spelling with the argument probe first, write to a new name
in a new directory, and compare the result before deciding
what, if anything, to replace.

In practice you will often get an exact name by pressing
Tab for completion rather than typing every punctuation
mark. A completion system may insert a backslash before
the blanks instead of quoting the entire name. Read the
completed command as written. Adding another set of quotes
around a backslash-escaped path could turn some of the
backslashes themselves into data. If editing the result is
more trouble than starting over, erase that operand and
write one plain quoted pathname. You do not need to keep
the particular syntax your completion system chose.

\begin{commandcard}{printf}
\CommandField{Use}{Display each argument without interpreting it as a format.}
\CommandSyntax|printf '<%s>\n' "$value"|
\CommandField{Check}{An empty string has an empty bracket pair; a newline in the value can span more than one visible line.}
\end{commandcard}

\section{Quote only the part that is data}
\label{sec:25-mixed}

Suppose the chair now wants every file with the literal base
name \texttt{Hall [draft]} and any extension. You mean the
brackets literally and the star as a pattern. Quoting the
whole word would protect the star as well, while leaving
the word unquoted would expose the brackets. Attach the
unquoted star to the quoted base without a space:

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' 'Hall [draft]'.*
printf '<%s>\n' 'Hall [draft].*'
\end{terminal}

In the fixture, the first line reports the real
\texttt{Hall [draft].txt}. The second always reports
\texttt{Hall [draft].*} regardless of what's in the folder.
The contrast is useful when you intend to select several
files but the preview shows something stranger. Preview
the names first; do not interpret a printed star as proof
of either failure or success. A star can be an ordinary
character in a filename, and some shells leave unmatched
patterns written as they were. Chapter~\ref{ch:26} tests
that case separately.

Quoting decisions are easiest to make locally. If a
section of a word contains a printed amount, protect that
amount. If another section is a variable to look up,
double-quote the lookup. If a third section is an
intentional glob, leave just its pattern characters
outside the quotes. You do not have to settle on one rule
for the entire word. You do need to distinguish the
spaces between arguments from spaces inside the data.

The same exercise works with the attachment's human
title instead of its file extension. Suppose a note
contains \texttt{Hall plan; check Monday}. Inside single
quotes the semicolon is ordinary punctuation. Outside
quotes it ends the first command and starts another.
There is no good reason to test the unquoted form if the
second part looks like a command you didn't intend to
run. Show the literal form with the probe instead:

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' 'Hall plan; check Monday'
\end{terminal}

This is why quoting is more than a cosmetic convention.
It's how you say that a character in the source is not
to be interpreted at that stage. A semicolon in a value
substituted from a variable is different from a semicolon
typed unquoted in the command itself: the shell had
already read the command boundaries before substituting
the value. Keep the variable expansion quoted anyway to
preserve its data boundary. No step in this exercise
needs to turn the attachment's title back into shell
instructions.

\section{Exercises and cleanup}

\begin{exercise}{Make two words one argument}
\label{ex:25-blank}
Print one argument for the name \texttt{Main hall.txt} using
each of the three quoting tools. You should see exactly one pair
of angle brackets per command. No files are needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' 'Main hall.txt'
printf '<%s>\n' "Main hall.txt"
printf '<%s>\n' Main\ hall.txt
\end{terminal}
Each run prints \texttt{<Main hall.txt>}. There is nothing to
remove.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Keep both special marks}
\label{ex:25-price}
Print \texttt{Marta's \$5 notes.txt} as one argument. Change the
double-quoted example for Marta's notes rather than using the
three-piece answer above. The amount and apostrophe must survive.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' "Marta's \$5 notes.txt"
\end{terminal}
Within double quotes, a backslash before the dollar sign stops it
from introducing an expansion. The backslash is not printed. No
files or variables are created.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Separate a glob from a literal read}
\label{ex:25-brackets}
In your practice directory, predict which line changes if
\texttt{Hall d.txt} is renamed to \texttt{Hall a.txt}. Then test
the pattern with the argument probe instead of reading the file.
Under both names the literal read should still return the draft.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' Hall\ [draft].txt
mv './Hall d.txt' './Hall a.txt'
printf '<%s>\n' Hall\ [draft].txt
cat 'Hall [draft].txt'
mv './Hall a.txt' './Hall d.txt'
\end{terminal}
The probe changes from \texttt{<Hall d.txt>} to \texttt{<Hall
a.txt>}. Both letters are in the bracket expression. Move the
fixture back, as shown, so it matches the cleanup list.
\end{solution}

To clean up, first use \texttt{pwd} and confirm that it is the
directory printed by setup. The list below is explicit: it does
not match anything else. The \texttt{-i} option asks for confirmation
before each removal. Then leave the practice directory before
asking \texttt{rmdir} to remove it; that command refuses to remove
it if anything remains. If a removal fails or was declined, inspect
the practice directory before deciding what remains to be removed.

\begin{terminal}
pwd
rm -i './Hall [draft].txt' './Hall d.txt' './-n'
cd / && rmdir "$practice"
exit
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Quote literal filenames, and use double quotes around substitutions that should produce one argument.
\item Adjacent pieces make one word unless you separate them outside the quotes.
\item Quoting protects against shell interpretation, not interpretation by the receiving command.
\end{takeaways}

